WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:13.210
I would like to move on now to the next item
on the Agenda, which is the draft directions

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:18.550
for the Health and Social Care Information
Centre. I will invite Tim to speak to us in

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:23.020
a moment, but just to record for the record
that there has been a letter received by the

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:28.960
Board from medConfidential. I will ask Tim
to address those issues in the letter, but

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:34.910
also, given the highly technical nature of
some of the issues, Tim, what I propose to

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:40.000
do is I will write formally back to medConfidential
giving assurance on all the points raised

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.480
by them, so please do not feel obliged to
go into every detail this morning.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:49.210
Tim Kelsey: fact, I think it follows very
nicely from that excellent presentation, because

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:54.680
one of the most important benefits of what
is called care.data, which at this stage is

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:58.489
a programme to link general practice data
with hospital data, will be to provide us

00:00:58.489 --> 00:01:03.930
with precisely those demographic benchmarks
that will help planning and commissioning

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:08.200
commissioners in different parts of the country
understand much more deeply what kinds of

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:16.790
patient behaviours are in common between them
and other localities. We lack that in a consistent

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:21.280
way across the country, and that is a very
important justification for this programme.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:27.259
Essentially, just to remind the Board, care.data
is about linking GP and hospital data, and

00:01:27.259 --> 00:01:34.080
we are doing that because we need to do it,
because without it patient care is not as

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:38.670
good as it could be. We know, for example,
that we have very serious issues in this country

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:42.970
around the late diagnosis of cancer, and we
know that people who are diagnosed with certain

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:48.159
kinds of cancer late are less likely to be
treated satisfactorily and survival rates

00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:52.799
are reduced as a result. If we are able to
link GP and hospital data, we will be able

00:01:52.799 --> 00:02:01.979
to support those GPs who are perhaps not identifying
cancer as early as they might to improve the

00:02:01.979 --> 00:02:04.049
survival rate for their own patients.

00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:08.420
I just want to make the point that this is
very much about clinical quality, patient

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:14.019
care, right here, right now, and also of course
it is about commissioning, transforming models

00:02:14.019 --> 00:02:17.660
of care as we understand, plans of integration
and how best to serve local communities. Finally,

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:25.230
it is also about research and how we can improve
the opportunity for the NHS to be at the cutting

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:30.770
edge of science and deliver to its patients
new generations of medicines and treatments.

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:37.540
For all those reasons, we are I hope certainly
very committed to care.data, but care.data

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:44.090
is also about testing a new standard of ensuring
that citizens are aware of the rights they

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:49.879
have in relation to use of their own data
when it is not for direct care. Thus, as the

00:02:49.879 --> 00:02:56.360
Board will know, the government has proposed
to introduce an opt-out; in other words, citizens

00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:00.500
will have the right to opt out of their data
being shared for reasons other than direct

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:05.130
care. Part of the process that is described
in these directions - and I will briefly outline

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:10.489
- is to make sure that we understand what
standard is appropriate for communicating

00:03:10.489 --> 00:03:16.890
that right of opt-out to patients. In doing
so, we are going to be setting a new bar for

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:20.680
data controllers in the NHS to meet.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:27.349
The programme was suspended last year, as
the Board will remember, because there were

00:03:27.349 --> 00:03:31.930
very legitimate concerns about the degree
of transparency of uses to which that data

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:37.690
would be put and also of the safeguards in
relation to confidentiality and ensuring people

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:39.670
were aware of their rights.

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:46.870
Therefore, for the last year or so, the team
- which involves teams from many different

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:52.530
organisations - has been involved in redesigning
the programme in a very co produced fashion.

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:57.810
More than 3,000 members of the general public
in nearly 200 events around the country have

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:04.519
contributed to the redesign of the whole scheme.
We are now in a position where GPs in four

00:04:04.519 --> 00:04:11.819
areas of the country have volunteered to test
the communications that have been developed,

00:04:11.819 --> 00:04:19.370
the new approach to ensuring people are aware
of their rights. Those areas comprise in total

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:25.630
six CCGs in four parts of the country, and
they are now planning to start those tests.

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:33.910
I want to remind the Board again and those
watching that this is just a test. We are

00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:37.470
trying to understand whether we have hit the
right standard, and the Secretary of State

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:45.720
has invited Dame Fiona Caldicott, the National
Data Guardian, to review progress in that

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:54.810
test in the early autumn, broadly. It will
be subsequent to her advice that we have achieved

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:59.360
an appropriate standard of communication with
the public, and also of course of this Board's

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:04.990
view and indeed the Secretary of State as
well, that we then proceed, if we do, to extraction

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:11.130
of data in those pathfinder areas, so that
we can demonstrate that there is real value

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:21.400
of the sort I have just described in health
and care. This is going to be quite a slow

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:26.150
but deliberate process to really establish
very transparently that we have achieved standards

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:32.520
that we can all agree are appropriate for
such an important project.

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:40.690
These directions relate only to the pathfinder
programme. The directions are to specify for

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:46.440
the Health and Social Care Information Centre
what data we would want to be extracted in

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:50.720
the event that Dame Fiona, this Board and
the Secretary of State have given their approval

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:57.039
for the extraction to proceed. I want to highlight
one or two quite important points about these

00:05:57.039 --> 00:06:00.310
directions for the Board's benefit.

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:07.660
The first thing to say is that we will be
testing in this process a citizen's right

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:13.080
to opt out. Just to be absolutely clear, this
means that if someone does exercise their

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:17.479
opt-out, no clinical data will be extracted
from their general practice. Just to make

00:06:17.479 --> 00:06:21.509
that point absolutely clearly.

00:06:21.509 --> 00:06:29.300
Secondly, given the importance of ensuring
public confidence as we begin to make the

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:36.020
case for data sharing in healthcare of this
sort, we have limited access to the data,

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:39.970
should it be extracted, at the end of the
pathfinder phase, in the pathfinder context,

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:44.520
to just four organisations. They are: NHS
England, Public Health England, the Health

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:49.350
and Social Care Information Centre and the
CQC. In addition, that those four organisations

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:54.910
will only be able to access it for analytic
purposes in a safe, secure environment that

00:06:54.910 --> 00:07:01.440
has been developed by the Information Centre.
Therefore, there will be no third party hosting

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:05.599
of the data that flows from the extraction.

00:07:05.599 --> 00:07:11.630
In the event that, again, Dame Fiona, this
Board, the Secretary of State and the Board

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:16.620
of the Information Centre is persuaded that
there is merit in the data analysis that proceeds

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:20.910
from the pathfinder extraction, and that we
have achieved an appropriate standard of what

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:25.539
is called fair processing, but explaining
to people their rights, then it may well be

00:07:25.539 --> 00:07:31.870
that we move to a programme of national rollout.
In that case, this Board will have to agree

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:35.750
a separate set of directions. So I just want
to make the point that this is not signing

00:07:35.750 --> 00:07:42.169
off anything other than a process to test
communications, and for a conditional approval

00:07:42.169 --> 00:07:45.490
on extracting data subject to the safeguards
I have just described.

00:07:45.490 --> 00:07:52.910
I hope that opens the ground for a conversation
if the Board wants clarification.

00:07:52.910 --> 00:07:59.500
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Tim. Are
there any comments from the Board?

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:06.400
Ciaran Devane: I Chair the Advisory Group,
as you know, so let me try to reflect where

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:12.139
I think we are coming from - not the Programme
Board, just to make sure we are clear on the

00:08:12.139 --> 00:08:12.679
distinction.

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:18.960
First of all, we are in a very different place
from where we were in the past. Taking things

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:25.060
slowly and deliberately, as Tim described
it, has been important, because it is the

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:31.099
realisation that this is new territory, this
is precedent. This is something we have to

00:08:31.099 --> 00:08:35.579
get right not only for this particular programme
and these pathfinders, but generically as

00:08:35.579 --> 00:08:43.099
well. That is the starting point of the Advisory
Group. Things like the safe setting, a physical

00:08:43.099 --> 00:08:50.350
building being built to make sure that everything
is as robust as possible for the pathfinders,

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:52.070
is a good thing to do, therefore.

00:08:52.070 --> 00:08:59.880
On the major issues around fair process, opt-out,
dissemination, I think we are in a much stronger

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:07.810
place than we were. We do have to recognise
that one of the consequences of having a pathfinder

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:13.870
approach is, as Tim described, directions
will change in the future. Therefore, if we

00:09:13.870 --> 00:09:18.990
are going to have a truly fair process, one
of the things we have to get right in the

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:24.180
pathfinders is making sure people understand
the directions we are operating under. For

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:30.149
example, the set of data that is extracted
and who can use it in the pathfinders will

00:09:30.149 --> 00:09:36.060
both be a subset of the data that will be
extracted and who can use it in the future.

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:40.750
It sounds like a very technical thing, but
if we are going to be true to this thing about

00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:46.620
fair process, then we have to make sure in
the pathfinders that we do that. For example,

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:50.810
at the Advisory Group we had last week, one
of the things we were saying was that in the

00:09:50.810 --> 00:10:00.680
communication of the process going forward
we have to make sure, for instance, that we

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:05.680
flag the fact that there will be further directions
and they will be changed in how we describe

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:08.829
these directions. Therefore, if somebody goes
on the website and it says, 'These are the

00:10:08.829 --> 00:10:15.670
directions,' we are overt in saying, 'And
subject to what Fiona Caldicott decides, these

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:24.570
things may move in a different way, but that
process will itself be transparent.'

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:31.620
For me, should we approve the directions?
Yes, we should, subject to a few things. Malcolm,

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:37.800
you have just offered to respond to the letter.
There are some typos that have been picked

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:43.790
up. We need to be clear that not all the pathfinders
are proceeding at the same pace. Leeds will

00:10:43.790 --> 00:10:49.070
be late; let us make sure that the schedule
reflects that. Let us make sure that we are

00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:56.420
absolutely sure that the things that will
change in future are flagged in an appropriate

00:10:56.420 --> 00:10:57.920
way.

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:05.390
I think the Advisory Group would say we are
in a very different place. The pathfinder

00:11:05.390 --> 00:11:12.079
approach is absolutely correct. We look forward
to making sure they operate as well as possible,

00:11:12.079 --> 00:11:19.829
that we extract the learnings from that and
then make sure that, if and when anything

00:11:19.829 --> 00:11:31.180
further happens, the learnings are truly built
into it. With those comments, for me, yes

00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:31.839
please.

00:11:31.839 --> 00:11:36.279
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Ciaran.

00:11:36.279 --> 00:11:45.430
Noel Gordon: I wanted to pick up on Ciaran's
point about the pathfinders, Tim, and let

00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:51.040
us accept the approach is the right approach.
What is it we seek to learn from the pathfinder

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:57.380
approach that will guide us in the decision
later about whether this becomes a national

00:11:57.380 --> 00:12:02.760
approach? What are the top three objectives
we seek to achieve?

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:11.850
Tim Kelsey: Dame Fiona has set a series of
standards that she expects the pathfinders

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:16.850
to demonstrate in relation, for example, to
supporting GPs to be able to discharge this

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:22.660
rather complex communication responsibility
that they have under the law in any case,

00:12:22.660 --> 00:12:29.339
on one level. On another level, how have we
demonstrated that people have adequately understood

00:12:29.339 --> 00:12:35.950
the right to opt out? How do we demonstrate
that as well? How do we make sure that populations

00:12:35.950 --> 00:12:42.260
that are relatively hard to reach, although
they may be listed with GPs, are also made

00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:48.700
aware of their opportunity to opt out? It
may help if I forwarded this to the Board.

00:12:48.700 --> 00:12:53.630
It is in the public domain, but I will forward
the letter she has written to me, as senior

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:57.639
responsible owner for the programme, describing
what sorts of standards she would expect us

00:12:57.639 --> 00:13:02.339
as a programme to meet, and we have discussed
it of course with the Advisory Group. That

00:13:02.339 --> 00:13:07.649
lays out a number of quite specific, tangible
objectives, which we then need to evaluate

00:13:07.649 --> 00:13:09.910
in light of the pathfinder experience.

00:13:09.910 --> 00:13:17.339
I am sorry, but I put my notes in the wrong
order. Could I just say how grateful we are

00:13:17.339 --> 00:13:22.350
to the pathfinders, the leadership, not just
the individual GP practices, but to the clinical

00:13:22.350 --> 00:13:29.860
commissioning group chairs and clinical leads
who have honestly really embraced this in

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:35.420
a way that has demonstrated to me the importance
of its absence to them in executing their

00:13:35.420 --> 00:13:40.380
own responsibilities. They have worked very
closely on working out how best they think

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:45.810
they can demonstrate those standards are being
achieved. There has been a great deal of work

00:13:45.810 --> 00:13:51.000
that has gone on with Healthwatch, with CCGs,
with local GPs just to try to make practical

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:57.160
some of the perfectly legitimate standards
that Dame Fiona has proposed. I will forward

00:13:57.180 --> 00:13:57.820
that letter.

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:03.800
If it is okay, Chairman, could I also thank
Ciaran for his great commitment in chairing

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:08.579
the Advisory Group, and also to all its members
who represent a very important set of voices,

00:14:08.579 --> 00:14:14.199
and to be fair, those voices have been challenging,
very robust. It is a very frank conversation,

00:14:14.199 --> 00:14:18.459
but thank you to them for helping us collectively,
I hope, make some progress.

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:25.080
Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Tim. Before I put
the question to the Board, I think it is important

00:14:25.089 --> 00:14:32.040
for us to be clear that this is a programme
in which we have invested a great deal of

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:38.639
time and thought in its development. We would
only do this if we believed that it was absolutely

00:14:38.639 --> 00:14:45.070
critical in the interests of patients that
this was something that would give us the

00:14:45.070 --> 00:14:49.959
information and the intelligence that we need
to more finely tune not only our commissioning

00:14:49.959 --> 00:14:59.579
practice, but also to get real time intelligence
about how patients' lives are lived, how treatments

00:14:59.579 --> 00:15:05.610
work and how we can better provide for their
care. I do not think that that is any longer

00:15:05.610 --> 00:15:12.149
a matter of huge controversy. The issue is
how do we sensitively attune ourselves to

00:15:12.149 --> 00:15:18.500
issues around patient data confidentiality,
and that is what the question is before the

00:15:18.500 --> 00:15:18.829
Board.

00:15:18.829 --> 00:15:24.690
What I would like to do, first of all, is
to propose that you will authorise me to thank

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:28.529
medConfidential for their comments and to
respond in detail to the issues that they

00:15:28.529 --> 00:15:34.320
have quite rightly raised. Secondly, that
you will approve in principle the directions

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:40.709
that are before you, but also delegate to
the Chief Executive and to me to do final

00:15:40.709 --> 00:15:44.910
approval on behalf of the Board once we have
had an opportunity to take into account the

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:49.850
comments from medConfidential and any other
issues, but the substance of the directions

00:15:49.850 --> 00:15:52.339
I would anticipate would remain unchanged.

00:15:52.339 --> 00:15:59.160
Is the Board content to proceed in that way?
Okay. Thank you very much. Thank you, Tim

00:15:59.160 --> 00:15:59.449
and Ciaran.

